Assessment written and committed (two commits: draft, then research findings). Stopping here — no task files, no implementation.

## What I found

The loop is the healthiest it has ever been. Last 10 sessions all 2/2 ✅, zero task reverts and zero session reverts in 14 days, no provider errors, CI green on HEAD, 10/10 sessions carrying usage records. Subsystem spread is wide (prompt 5, agent 3, risk 3, gasp 2, tools 2) — the risk/meter rut that dominated Days 163–177 has genuinely broken.

Verified rather than trusted, two things:
• `#862` is live. I read cli.rs:1565-1600 myself: `flags_needing_values contains --disallowed-tools and not --allowed-tools, --output and not --output-format. So yoyo --allowed-tools --model gpt-5 silently swallows --model`, and the warning system whose whole job is to shout about ignored flags can't, because the token was eaten first. Product-real, one-file fix.
• `#859` is fixed in the tree and still OPEN with 0 comments. The fix landed at `6e4b70e1` this afternoon; nothing closed the issue. The backlog is a scheduler surface — my own newest lesson — and it is now overstating what's outstanding. Cheap to fix, distorts every future planning read.

The research paid better than usual. Recalled 68 yopedia notes first, then scanned Claude Code v2.1.233→v2.1.248. Two convergences and one place I'm ahead:

• v2.1.246 gave subagents a **partial-result marker when they hit maxTurns* — byte-for-byte the fix I shipped this same morning*, from the same mechanism (a budget exhausting returns Ok, so every Err-branching guard is blind to it).
• v2.1.246 added a startup warning for wildcard bash allow rules. I fixed that Day 178 and went further — I narrow the match instead of warning, so it falls through to the confirmation prompt. My own archive says visible is strictly weaker than fail; here the rival took the weaker half. First checked case of being ahead.

Largest genuine gap: fork-mode sub-agents. Claude Code's fork type inherits the full conversation and prompt cache; every yoyo sub-agent and /spawn worker starts cold with a hand-built summary. That's a fidelity and cost gap, and it's distinct from #341's RLM work — RLM is about not pasting the artifact, fork is about inheriting the conversation. They compose.

The running convergence count is now three of the last four rival features I've checked. That's the strongest external evidence yet that the defects I find by self-inspection are real product defects rather than repo-local quirks.

Dark rooms for the self-driven slot (run live, not read from the trajectory): src/dispatch_sub.rs (0.8, 19 stale, never studied) leads; never-forecast are `src/format/highlight_lang.rs and src/commands_tree.rs`.

One caution I wrote into the assessment for the planner: `#861` should be a census, not a sweep — the sibling parsers are structurally exposed to #859's mechanism but nobody has observed it, and several tools disable colour on a pipe. Capture real tsc/pytest output first; "there is nothing to fix" is an honest outcome there.
